u} \approx 0.1 	ext{ eV} \iff M_R \approx 10^{14} 	ext{ GeV}$, is a numerical benchmark for the Type-I seesaw mechanism. It arises from the general formula $m_{
u} \approx  rac{m_D^2}{M_R}$, where the Dirac mass term $m_D$ is generated by electroweak symmetry breaking, $m_D = y_{
u}v$. Here, $v \approx 246 	ext{ GeV}$ is the Higgs vacuum expectation value and $y_{
u}$ is the neutrino Yukawa coupling. By assuming $y_{
u} \sim 1$ (i.e., the coupling is of the same order as for other fundamental fermions), the formula becomes $m_{
u} \approx  rac{(y_{
u}v)^2}{M_R}$. Plugging in the observed scale for neutrino mass, $m_{
u} \approx 0.1 	ext{ eV}$, one can solve for the required mass of the heavy right-handed neutrino, $M_R$: $M_R \approx  rac{(1 \cdot 246 	ext{ GeV})^2}{0.1 	ext{ eV}} \approx 6 	imes 10^{14} 	ext{ GeV}$. The symbol '$\iff$' here signifies a direct implication within this specific model and its assumptions, not a universal logical equivalence.

